---
/**
 * Bend's GPU path against hand-written Metal and CUDA. The point of the page:
 * native kernels are faster, and say by how much; Bend's single source still
 * outruns every CPU path, runs on both GPUs, and carries the proofs.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import BarChart from '../components/BarChart.astro';
import DataTable from '../components/DataTable.astro';
import Note from '../components/Note.astro';
import { tableView, gbPerSecond, parseBytes, splitInput } from '../lib/model';
import { fmtRate } from '../lib/format';
import { gpuMachines, gpuShares } from '../lib/more';
import { lawCount } from '../lib/proofs';
import { story } from '../lib/news';
import { blobUrl, treeUrl } from '../lib/site';

const links = [
  { href: '#summary', label: 'Summary' },
  ...gpuMachines.map((m) => ({ href: `#${m.id}`, label: m.title.replace(/^NVIDIA /, '') })),
  { href: '#why', label: 'Why Bend' },
];

const pct = (x: number | undefined) => (x === undefined ? 'n/a' : `${Math.round(x * 100)}%`);

/** Bend's fastest GPU rate on the biggest input, and where. */
const peak = gpuMachines
  .map((m) => {
    const row = m.gpu.rows[m.gpu.rows.length - 1]!;
    const ms = row.values.get('bend_gpu');
    const bytes = parseBytes(splitInput(row.label).size);
    return { title: m.title, input: row.label, rate: ms && bytes ? gbPerSecond(bytes, ms) : 0 };
  })
  .sort((a, b) => b.rate - a.rate)[0]!;

const refs = [story('0006'), story('0011')].filter((s) => s !== undefined);
---

<Base title="GPU: Bend against native kernels" path="gpu/" description="turboblake's GPU path, BLAKE3 written once in Bend2, against hand-written Metal and CUDA kernels on an Apple M5 Pro, an NVIDIA B200 and an L4.">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" links={links} />

  <section class="intro" id="summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">GPU</p>
      <h1 id="summary-title">Bend on the GPU, next to hand-written kernels</h1>
      <div class="prose lede">
        <p>
          turboblake's GPU path is BLAKE3 written once in Bend2 and compiled by Bend for Metal and for CUDA. Here it runs on the same bytes as hand-written
          Metal and CUDA kernels (<a href={treeUrl('bend/ref')}><code>bend/ref</code></a>), which give the same digest. The hand-written kernels are faster.
          On the biggest input Bend reaches{' '}
          {gpuShares.map((s, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 && (i === gpuShares.length - 1 ? ' and ' : ', ')}
              <strong>{pct(s.share)}</strong> of native speed on the {s.title}
            </>
          ))}.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is still fast enough for any job that has to read its input first: <strong>{fmtRate(peak.rate)} GB/s</strong> on the {peak.title} for
          {' '}{peak.input}, more than an NVMe SSD or a 100-gigabit link can deliver. And it is the same source as the CPU engine, with {lawCount} laws about it
          checked by Bend's proof kernel.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="shares" role="list">
        {
          gpuShares.map((s) => (
            <li class="card">
              <span class="share-n num">{pct(s.share)}</span>
              <span class="share-of">of native speed</span>
              <span class="share-where">
                {s.title}, {s.input}
              </span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  {
    gpuMachines.map((m, i) => (
      <Section id={m.id} kicker={m.machine} title={`${m.title}: Bend against native`} tone={i % 2 === 0 ? 'alt' : 'plain'}>
        <Fragment slot="lede">
          <p>
            GB/s from the input size and the measured time, so longer bars are faster. The speedup on each Bend bar is against the fastest native kernel on that
            row; "Bend's leaves" runs the native kernel in the shape Bend's tree gives the GPU, one thread per leaf, which is how much of the gap is the shape
            and how much is the code.
          </p>
        </Fragment>
        <Note text={m.note} />
        <div class="charts">
          {m.gpuCharts.map((c) => (
            <BarChart chart={{ ...c, title: c.title ?? 'GPU' }} />
          ))}
        </div>
        <DataTable id={`${m.id}-gpu-table`} view={tableView(m.gpu, `${m.title}, GPU, times in ${m.gpu.unit} with GB/s below each time.`, { throughput: true })} />
        {m.cpu && (
          <>
            <h3 class="sub">The CPU, for scale</h3>
            <div class="charts">
              {m.cpuCharts.map((c) => (
                <BarChart chart={{ ...c, title: c.title ?? 'CPU' }} />
              ))}
            </div>
            <DataTable id={`${m.id}-cpu-table`} view={tableView(m.cpu, `${m.title}, CPU, times in ${m.cpu.unit} with GB/s below each time.`, { throughput: true })} />
          </>
        )}
      </Section>
    ))
  }

  <Section id="why" kicker="Why Bend" title="Not the fastest way, and good enough">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>A hand-written kernel is the upper bound. Bend trades some of it for things a kernel per platform cannot give:</p>
    </Fragment>
    <ul class="reasons" role="list">
      <li class="card">
        <h3>One source</h3>
        <p>
          <a href={blobUrl('bend/blake3.bend')}><code>bend/blake3.bend</code></a> is the CPU engine (eight lanes in SIMD registers), the Metal and CUDA
          path, and the JavaScript build. There is no kernel per platform to keep in step.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3>Proved</h3>
        <p>
          {lawCount} laws about that code, the tree's shape and its outboards among them, are checked by Bend's proof kernel on every change (<a
            href={blobUrl('bend/LAWS.bend')}><code>LAWS.bend</code></a
          >). The native kernels are tested, not proved.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3>Where the gap is</h3>
        <p>
          A native kernel picks its memory layout and how many chunks a thread hashes; Bend's runtime schedules a tree of tasks. Two write-ups measure
          both:{' '}
          {refs.map((r, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 && ' and '}
              <a href={r.writeUp}>{r.headline}</a>
            </>
          ))}.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3>When it matters</h3>
        <p>
          A one-shot command hashes on the CPU: setting up the GPU's heap costs more than the whole CPU run. The GPU pays off in a long-running process that
          keeps its heap, and for data already in memory.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="aside prose">
      On NVIDIA hardware the runs are on <a href="https://modal.com">Modal</a>, whose sandbox grants no managed allocation over 1 GiB; Bend's CUDA heap is built
      there from 1 GiB pieces placed back to back (<a href={blobUrl('bend/bench/modal_gpu.py')}><code>bend/bench/modal_gpu.py</code></a>).
    </p>
  </Section>

  <SiteFooter slot="footer" />
</Base>

<style>
  .intro {
    padding-block: calc(var(--header-h) + clamp(3rem, 2rem + 4vw, 5.5rem)) clamp(3rem, 2rem + 3vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .intro h1 {
    max-width: 20ch;
    margin-block: 0.6rem 1.2rem;
    font-size: clamp(2.125rem, 1.4rem + 3vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  .lede {
    max-width: 44rem;
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
  }

  .lede p + p {
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  .shares {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 13rem), 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 2.25rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .shares li {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.25rem;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.25rem;
  }

  .share-n {
    color: var(--ion);
    font-size: 2.25rem;
    font-weight: 650;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
  }

  .share-of {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .share-where {
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  .charts {
    display: grid;
    gap: 3rem;
  }

  .sub {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    font-size: 1.125rem;
  }

  .reasons {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .reasons li {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    padding: 1.4rem 1.35rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .reasons h3 {
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
  }

  .reasons p {
    color: var(--ink-2);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }

  .aside {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
</style>
